===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|libbe|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{term|lybb||medicine, drug, potion, poison, charm|lang=ang}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{recons|lubją||wort, herb, drug, poison|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{recons|lewbʰ-|lang=ine-pro}}, {{recons|lewb-||to peel, break, damage|lang=ine-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{recons|lew-||to cut, remove, prune, separate|lang=ine-pro}}. Cognate with {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Luppe|lang=de}}, {{term|Lüppe||salve, ointment, plant juice, medicine, magic|lang=de}}, {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|lyf||medicine, drug|lang=is}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|UK|_|dialectal|Scotland|lang=en}} A [[potion]]; [[magic]] [[potion]]; [[charm]].

===Etymology 3===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|libben|lang=enm}}, related to {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|lubben||to castrate, emasculate|lang=dum}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|libbe||a steer|lang=nl}}, {{term|lubbert||a eunuch|lang=nl}}. Further relation uncertain. Possibly related to {{etyl|ang|-}} {{recons|lybbian|lybbian|to doctor|lang=ang}}, from {{term|lybb|lang=ang}} (see above); or perhaps related to {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term|lappa|lang=ang}}, {{term|læppa||lappet, piece, section, lobe, portion, district|lang=ang}}. More at {{l|en|lop}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|libb|ed}}

# {{context|transitive|UK|_|dialectal|Northern England|Scotland|lang=en}} To [[geld]]; [[castrate]]; [[emasculate]] (usually said of animals).

